{{also|Welch|welch'}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Alternative form of {{m|en|welsh}}, from {{m|en|Welsh}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/wɛlt͡ʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-welch.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛltʃ|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[person]] who [[default]]s on an [[obligation]], especially a [[small]] one.
#: {{syn|en|welcher}}
#: {{cot|en|moocher|pincher}}
#: {{ux|en|She's a '''welch'''. That watering-can isn't hers: I lent it to her three years ago.}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[fail]] to [[repay]] a small debt.
#: {{cot|en|mooch#Verb|pinch#Verb}}
# To [[fail]] to [[fulfill]] an obligation.
#* '''1992''', {{w|Bo Goldman}}, ''{{w|Scent of a Woman (1992 film)|Scent of a Woman}}'' (film)
#*: I '''welched'''. I'm a welcher. Didn't I tell you?
#* '''1973''', {{w|Mario Puzo}}, {{w|Francis Ford Coppola}}, ''{{w|The Godfather Part II}}'' (screenplay, second draft)
#*: MICHAEL: Clemenza promised Rosato three territories in the Bronx after he died, and then you took over and '''welched'''.

